                <fr:title text="Continuations and Sharding">Continuations and Sharding</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    Splitting a long execution into segments that can be proved independently and in parallel. The mechanism that makes zkVM proving a distributed job rather than a single machine's problem.
</html:p>
                <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>Segmenting a trace: what state must be carried across a boundary, and how it is committed.</html:li>
        <html:li>The consistency argument between shards, especially for <fr:link href="/dhsorens-003U/" title="Memory Checking in zkVMs" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-003U/" display-uri="dhsorens-003U" type="local">memory</fr:link>.</html:li>
        <html:li>Aggregating shard proofs into one, and the recursion tree's shape.</html:li>
        <html:li>How SP1 and RISC Zero each do it, and what their differences imply.</html:li>
        <html:li>The soundness surface this adds — a shard boundary is a place to forget a constraint.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
